## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a low-level, lock-free queue system with NUMA locality, ABA avoidance, and fair work-stealing, plus concrete microbenchmarking plans.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring buffers with sequence counters for ABA avoidance
- NUMA-aware locality and cross-node stealing
- Per-thread local queues and a global steal pool
- Cache-line alignment and memory fencing
- Microbenchmark design for concurrency and contention

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: ring enqueue/dequeue with sequence counters
class Slot { constructor(){ this.seq=0; this.data=null } }
class Ring { constructor(n){ this.size=n; this.mask=n-1; this.buf=Array(n).fill(null).map(()=>new Slot()); this.head=0; this.tail=0 }
  enqueue(x){ const pos = this.head & this.mask; const s = this.buf[pos]; if (s.seq !== this.head) return false; s.data = x; s.seq = this.head + 1; this.head++; return true }
  dequeue(){ const pos = this.tail & this.mask; const s = this.buf[pos]; if (s.seq !== this.tail+1) return null; const v = s.data; s.data=null; s.seq = this.tail + 2; this.tail++; return v }
}}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to more NUMA nodes and maintain fairness? 
- What backoff strategy and memory ordering would you choose under high contention?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design robust, low-latency IPC primitives under NUMA-aware multi-producer/multi-consumer contention with precise memory ordering requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free design, with ABA avoidance via per-slot sequence counters
- NUMA-aware memory placement to minimize cross-socket memory traffic
- Shadow consumer tail optimization to reduce remote cache-line access
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Memory ordering semantics (acquire/release, memory_order_acq_rel)

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t sequence;
    char data[56];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    slot_t slots[N] __attribute__((aligned(64)));
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
    volatile uint64_t shadow_tail;
} mpmc_queue_t;

static inline int enqueue(mpmc_queue_t* q, const void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(s->data, data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}

static inline int dequeue(mpmc_queue_t* q, void* data) {
    uint64_t slot = __atomic_fetch_add(&q->head, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL);
    slot_t* s = &q->slots[slot & (N - 1)];
    while (s->sequence != slot + 1) { /* spin */ }
    memcpy(data, s->data, 56);
    __atomic_store(&s->sequence, slot + N, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    return 0;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Outline

To isolate NUMA effects under asymmetric producer/consumer loads:

1. **Setup**: Run producers on socket 0, consumers on socket 1; measure round-trip latency and throughput.
2. **Workload injection**: Vary producer/consumer ratios (e.g., 10:1, 1:10, 5:5) to create asymmetric contention.
3. **Metrics**: Capture remote memory accesses via `numactl --hardware`, cache-miss ratios via `perf stat`, and per-socket latency distributions.
4. **Validation**: Compare interleaved vs. local-only memory placement to quantify NUMA effects; verify shadow tail reduces remote cache-line bouncing.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamentals of memory allocators, pointer arithmetic, alignment, and simple free-list management in a low-level setting. It also checks the ability to reason about initialization and fragmentation.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator
- Free-list embedded pointers
- Heap initialization
- O(1) allocation/deallocation
- Fragmentation considerations with fixed-size pools

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define HEAP_SIZE (64*1024)
#define BLOCK_SIZE 32
#define BLOCKS (HEAP_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE)

static uint8_t heap[HEAP_SIZE];
static uint32_t free_head;

void init_heap(){
  free_head = 0;
  for(size_t i=0;i<BLOCKS;++i){
    uint32_t next = (i==BLOCKS-1) ? UINT32_MAX : (i+1);
    *((uint32_t*)(heap + i*BLOCK_SIZE)) = next;
  }
}

void* alloc_block(){
  if(free_head==UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t off = free_head;
  free_head = *((uint32_t*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE));
  return (void*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE);
}

void free_block(void* p){
  size_t off = ((uint8_t*)p - heap) / BLOCK_SIZE;
  *((uint32_t*)(heap + off*BLOCK_SIZE)) = free_head;
  free_head = off;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to multiple threads? Consider a per-thread arena or atomic free-list head.

